WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.349 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  you're seven years old  sitting in a classroom with 50 kids you  don't know  the teacher is talking  about  you see her point at the blackboard  you hear her voice  but you do not understand a word  and you have just a few months  to pass an exam  in this unknown language  that's no nightmare  that was my childhood growing up in  india  moving every few years  from a state where everyone spoke hindi  to a state where everyone spoke bangla  to a state where everyone spoke uriah  at seven  when everything seemed impossible  i wanted to cry  i wanted to run away  i wanted to quit  but i tried my best  to push away my doubts and fears and  power through  at nine when we moved again  it got just a bit easier  even though i could not talk to my  classmates for a while we connected over  cricket and volleyball  we shared delicious parathas over lunch  and we teamed up at scouts after school  by 11  i was almost looking forward to the next  move  the promise of new friends  new rivals  new foods  far outweighed the miscommunication  living this experience as a child was  transformative  over time  i got better and better  at shoving away my doubts and fears and  powering  through eventually the move stopped  but left behind was this strange  tingling openness  to try new things  you've heard the cliches take a leap of  faith  jump into the deep end and learn to swim  fake it till you make it  nobody told me any of that back then  for me it was simple  the discomfort goes away if you just  power through  and power through i did  before coming to the gsb i spent 15  years at a b2b tech company  starting as an engineer in india  and pivoting over 10 times  in those 15 years across roles  functions  teams even geographies  i went out of my way to master new  skills  working during the day  and starting evenings and weekends for  three years to get a business degree to  pivot to marketing  learning spanish for presentations and  deals in mexico  traveling the world meeting customers to  understand  their pain points always at work i would  settle for nothing less than exceeding  expectations  powering through served me quite well  until it didn't  when i got promoted to a manager i put  the same pressure on my team to exceed  expectations that i put on myself  i was sure  they would get through their discomfort  by powering through  so when the first 360 reviews came in i  was excited  i remember running upstairs this corner  conference room with frosted glass walls  to read in private  and i opened my reviews one by one the  first one read  switch it should avoid being so pushy  it was as if those frosted glass walls  shattered around me  i could feel my energy go away who could  be this malcontent  with a little more hope i opened the  second review and it read  such it should trust and empower the  team more and let go of the details  oh no  i could feel the blood drain down my  head as i slouched into my chair  the reviews did not get any better from  there  by the time  i got through them i felt like that  seven-year-old again  i wanted to cry  i want it to run away  i want it to quit  and among those lines one line stayed  with me  such it needs to pause and smell a few  more roses  was i missing something  i realized  not immediately but over the next  several months  that all those doubts and fears i had  shoved away were still out there in the  shadows  powering through  had a cost that i hadn't reckoned with  before  i had moved way too often to form  long-term and deep friendships  i felt this awkward distance with people  that others perceived as me being  disconnected  the idea of home for me was not tied to  a particular place but only  to a close group of people  eventually the 11 year old in me took  over  i powered through once again and i  reached out to a trusted mentor and  coach for help  who convinced me to pause and reflect on  the impact i was having on my team  gradually i learned to embrace the truth  that each member of my team had more  years of experience than i did  i learned  to align each member's individual goals  with that of the team and the  organization  i organized team building exercises  where there were no hierarchies i leaned  in to listen more  to gain consensus to understand their  perspectives  but above everything  i stopped expecting them to power  through every discomfort the way i did  standing here now  i know i talked to an audience of people  who powers through  who shoves away  doubts and fears who takes leap after  leap after leap  and even though it works for you even  though it works for us  we need to remember as we try to change  lives organizations and the world  that not all the people we lead  operate the same way  we need to have empathy and  understanding for the people on our  teams  who may respond to discomfort  differently we cannot push them as hard  as we push ourselves  it is also important to know that how we  act sets an example  for our colleagues  for our employees and as i recently  realized even  for our families  the other day my wife minal and i were  discussing our next steps after  graduation  we lived in atlanta before we got to  stanford should we go back or should we  stay here  so i turned to my almost nine-year-old  son  ryan  what do you think atlanta or california  dad neither  i think i want to try some place new  at that moment i felt overwhelmed inside  so proud of it of his adventurous spirit  much like me when i was nine  yet concerned that he might also  miss a place that felt like home  there is a balance  between always powering through  and pausing to smell the roses  between moving on  and enjoying  where you are and what you have  i wish i could tell you exactly where  that balance lies but i'm still trying  to find it  so my ask of you today is this  keep in mind not only the benefits but  also the costs of always powering  through  and try to find a balance  that works for you and just as  importantly for the people you lead  [Applause]  [Music]

